+For a small-business researcher deciding where to try AI, the useful starting point is one bounded customer question, retained research material, and a named person responsible for interpreting the result. This article’s argument is that a pilot should measure the work required to produce trustworthy decision support, including preparation, checking, and maintenance. A finished summary is only an intermediate output. The evidence reviewed here supports this evaluation approach; it does not establish that AI will save your business time or money.
 
 Nielsen Norman Group distinguishes asking AI to organize research evidence already collected from asking it to generate evidence the researcher does not possess. [claim:03-C01] Its [empathy-mapping guidance](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/empathy-mapping) also requires each note to connect to a specific research artifact, with unsupported notes labeled as assumptions. For your pilot, that suggests choosing material you can inspect: interview transcripts, qualitative survey responses, or support records. Ask the tool to organize that material while preserving the route back to it.
 
